  "problem": "${(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x+2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1-2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x \\\\  & =1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x+2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1-2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x \\\\  & =2+{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=2+{tan}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
